# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this repository carries: the analysis code and the published page.
# Not the data, not what the models produced from it, and not the working notes.
#
# Three reasons, in order of how badly each would go wrong:
#
#   1. NAMED EMPLOYERS. Several files rank real companies by how much they
#      repeat their own job adverts. The observation is factual and drawn from
#      public adverts, but publishing a browsable list of 311 named firms is a
#      commercial decision, not a technical one. The public page ships with the
#      cloud anonymised; the named build stays local.
#
#   2. RAW ADVERT TEXT. Review sheets, intruder scans and cluster member lists
#      quote requirement text verbatim from a licensed export that is explicitly
#      not redistributed here. Aggregates are fine; the sentences are not ours
#      to publish.
#
#   3. SIZE. The ESCO source file is 1.3 GB and two embedding stores are ~290 MB
#      each. GitHub refuses anything over 100 MB, so without these rules the
#      first push fails outright rather than politely.
#
# Everything excluded below regenerates from the scripts that are committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ESCO source and its derived vectors — 1.3 GB + 291 MB, and the RDF is EU open
# data that should be downloaded rather than mirrored
week 2/esco/

# ---- data, by extension rather than by location ----
# The per-folder rules below this block were all written after the file they
# needed to catch already existed, which is the wrong order: a table written to
# a directory nobody thought of is not covered. These four rules do not care
# where the file lands.
#
# Every one of these regenerates from the committed scripts. If an aggregate
# table ever needs to be published, un-ignore that one file explicitly —
# `!week 4/data/whatever.csv` — rather than widening the rule.
*.csv
*.npz
*.parquet
*.jsonl

# The sensitive material is mostly .csv and .jsonl, so the extension rules above
# already hold it: employer rankings (`employer_templates`,
# `employer_leave_one_out`), and every file quoting advert text verbatim
# (`intruder_*`, `merge_*`, `*_corrections`). Listed here only in the formats the
# extension rules do not reach.
week 4/data/chart4_employers.json
week 4/dashboard.html
week 3/review_sets.json
week 3/week3-review-desk.html
week 3/dense_groups_review.md

# analysis output — aggregate and harmless, but regenerable, and the repo holds
# the code that makes it rather than the results it made
week 3/*.json
week 3/*.txt

# chart datasets are built by week 4/build_chart_data.py
week 4/data/
week 4/articles/data/

# article drafts stay local until they are published. The scripts and templates
# that make them are committed; the built pages are not, so an unfinished draft
# cannot be read from the repository before it is finished.
week 4/articles/*.html
!week 4/articles/*_template.html

# internal working notes: the handover file, the defect register, unpublished
# drafts, planning documents and speaking notes
CLAUDE.md
karimi-findings.md
karimi-defects.md
karimi-draft-publication.md
karimi-dashboard.md
week 3/discorso-settimana3.md
week 3/week3-briefing.html
.superseded/

# the week write-ups, held back until they are actually published. Every one of
# these has been rewritten several times as measurements corrected earlier ones,
# and a half-published record is worse than none: a reader landing on a figure
# that a later section retracts has no way to know which one stands.
week 1/karimi-week1.md
week 2/karimi-week2.md
week 2/karimi-week2-plan.md
